NAME
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The name "Geomorphic Reclamation Designer" uses "geomorphic reclamation", the
standard descriptive term for this discipline in the peer-reviewed literature.

It resembles the title of "Geomorphic Reclamation Design" (Bugosh & Martin Duque,
2024), which is this project's primary scientific reference. To be explicit:

  - this software is NOT the official companion software of that book;
  - it is NOT authored, reviewed or endorsed by its authors;
  - the book is cited here as a SOURCE, in the same way any scientific work is
    cited by an independent implementation of the methods it describes.

Any errors in this implementation are the responsibility of this project's
authors, not of the authors of the works cited below.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SCIENTIFIC REFERENCES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The method implemented here is described in the published literature. The
equations used by the engine are traceable, one by one, to the citations listed
in context/01_metodo_geofluv.md and verified by tests/test_libro.py.

  Bugosh, N. & Martin Duque, J.F. (2024). Geomorphic Reclamation Design.
  Williams, G.P. (1986). River meanders and channel size.
      Journal of Hydrology 88, 147-164.
  Rosgen, D. (1996). Applied River Morphology.
  Dunne, T. & Leopold, L.B. (1978). Water in Environmental Planning.
  Martin Duque, J.F. et al. (2017). Geomorphic reclamation for reestablishment
      of landform stability at a watershed scale in mined sites: El Machorro
      Mine, Spain. Ecological Engineering.
  Bugosh, N. & Epp, E. (2019). Evaluating sediment production from native and
      fluvial geomorphic-reclamation watersheds at La Plata Mine.
      Catena 174, 383-398.

